D = {
 "Hostile [Attitude]|XPHB": "A Hostile creature views you unfavorably. You have Disadvantage on an ability check to influence a Hostile creature.",
 "Human Languages|SCAG": "Optional rule. If the DM allows it, a human from an ethnic group with its own language knows that language as well as Common, as a free extra language. Examples: Bedine speak Midani, Calishite speak Alzhedo, Damaran speak Damaran, Ffolk speak Waelan, Rashemi speak Rashemi, Shou speak Shou, Tuigan speak Tuigan, Turami speak Turmic, Ulutiun speak Uluik. Most are written in Thorass, a few in Draconic, Dethek, or Espruar.",
 "Indifferent [Attitude]|XPHB": "An Indifferent creature has no desire to help or hinder you. Indifferent is the default attitude of a Monster.",
 "Initiative Variants|DMG": "Optional rules. Initiative Score: no roll, initiative equals 10 + Dexterity modifier. Side Initiative: each side rolls one d20 with no modifiers, the highest wins, ties reroll, and a side's members act in any order. Speed Factor: choose an action, then roll initiative every round. Modifiers: subtract spell level, heavy melee -2, light or finesse melee +2, two-handed -2, loading ranged -5, Tiny +5, Small +2, Large -2, Huge -5, Gargantuan -8. Apply each once.",
 "Injuries|DMG": "Optional rule. A creature may suffer a lingering injury after a critical hit, dropping to 0 hit points without dying, or failing a death save by 5 or more. Roll d20: 1 lose an eye, 2 lose an arm or hand, 3 lose a foot or leg, 4 limp, 5-7 internal injury (DC 15 Constitution save each combat action), 8-10 broken ribs (DC 10), 11-13 horrible scar, 14-16 festering wound (hit point maximum falls 1 per day), 17-20 minor scar.",
 "Level Advancement without XP|DMG": "Optional rule. The DM decides when characters level up. Session-based: 2nd level after the first session, 3rd after one more, 4th after two more, then two or three sessions per level, assuming four-hour sessions. Story-based: award levels when the party reaches significant campaign goals. This helps when a campaign has little combat or so much that tracking XP is tiresome.",
 "Line [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "A Line extends from a point of origin in a straight path. The effect specifies its length and width. The point of origin is not in the area unless the creator decides otherwise.",
 "Loyalty|DMG": "Optional rule for NPC party members. Loyalty runs 0 to 20. The maximum equals the party's highest Charisma score and the start is half of that. It rises 1d4 when the party aids its bond goal, treats it well, or rescues it. It falls 1d4 for acts against its alignment or bond and 2d4 for selfish abuse or endangerment. At 10 or higher the NPC risks life and limb. At 0 it leaves or opposes the party.",
 "Madness|DMG": "Optional rule. Spells, diseases, poisons, planar effects, and artifacts can cause madness, usually after a Wisdom or Charisma save. Short-term madness lasts 1d10 minutes. Long-term madness lasts 1d10 times 10 hours. Indefinite madness is a flaw lasting until cured. Each type has a d100 table. Calm emotions suppresses madness. Lesser restoration cures short-term and long-term madness. Greater restoration or stronger magic cures indefinite madness. Remove curse or dispel evil and good may also help.",
 "Massive Damage|DMG": "Optional rule. A creature that takes damage from a single source equal to or greater than half its hit point maximum must succeed on a DC 15 Constitution save or roll d10 on the System Shock table. Results run from dropping to 0 hit points, to being stunned until the end of its next turn, to losing reactions until the end of its next turn.",
 "Milestones|DMG": "Optional rule. Award XP when characters complete milestones, such as a goal toward finishing the adventure, a hidden location or key information, or an important destination. A major milestone counts as a hard encounter and a minor one as an easy encounter. You can add small rewards like a short rest, one Hit Die or low-level spell slot back, or recharged magic item uses.",
 "Mixing Potions|DMG": "Variant rule. When a character mixes two potions, roll d100 on the Potion Miscibility table, and roll again for each extra potion. 01 explosion: 6d10 force damage to the mixer and 1d10 force to creatures within 5 feet. 02-08 ingested poison. 09-15 both lose effect. 16-25 one loses effect. 26-35 both halved. 36-90 normal. 91-99 one doubled. 00 one works permanently.",
 "Morale|DMG": "Optional rule. A creature may flee when surprised, first reduced to half its hit points or fewer, or unable to harm the enemy. A group may flee when all are surprised, its leader falls or is removed, or it is halved with no enemy losses. Make a DC 10 Wisdom save for the creature or leader, with disadvantage or automatic failure if overwhelmed. On a failure it flees by the fastest route or surrenders if it cannot escape.",
 "More Difficult Identification|DMG": "Variant rule. Remove the option to learn a magic item's properties during a short rest. Instead, require the identify spell, experimentation, or both.",
 "Multiclassing|PHB": "Optional rule. When you level up, you may take a level in a new class if you have 13 or higher in the prerequisite ability of both your current and new class. XP and proficiency bonus use total character level. Same-size Hit Dice pool together. You gain only some new-class proficiencies. Extra Attack and Unarmored Defense don't stack. Spell slots add full caster levels, half paladin and ranger levels, and a third of Eldritch Knight and Arcane Trickster levels.",
 "Mysterious Islands|GOS": "Optional rules to generate islands. An island is roughly 1d6 miles long and 1d6 miles wide, with a habitat fitting its climate or a conjured material. Choose or roll a d6 theme: Alien, Cursed, Hostile, Sanctum, Welcoming, or Wild. Each theme has tables for its leader, inhabitants, reactions, curses or features, and story hooks. Cursed islands can raise specters, cause exhaustion, blind visitors, or add madness.",
 "New Ability Scores: Honor and Sanity|DMG": "Optional rule adding Honor and Sanity scores. Add an 11 to the standard array, 3 points to point buy, or one more roll per added score. Monsters without them use Charisma for Honor and Wisdom for Sanity. Honor can't rise by normal increases. The DM changes it by 1 after an adventure based on conduct, within 1 to 20. Sanity saves resist madness. Long-term or indefinite madness lowers Sanity by 1, and greater restoration restores it.",
 "Ocean Environs|GOS": "Optional rules for sea features. Blue holes hide monsters or treasure. Coral reefs deal 3 (1d6) slashing damage per 5 feet of forced movement. Currents add or subtract 1d6 or 1d4 - 1 miles per hour of pace. Light zones: sunlight to 650 feet, twilight to 1,000 feet, midnight below. Eldritch mist, kelp forests, kraken's graves, lure lights, magical storms, sandbars, sapping snow, shipwrecks, and whirlpools each add hazards with saves and checks, usually DC 10 to 15.",
 "Officers and Crew|GOS": "A ship needs a captain, and may add a first mate, bosun, quartermaster, surgeon, and cook. The crew's quality score starts at +4 and ranges from -10 to +10, shifted by casualties, hardship, morale, and health. Loyalty equals quality + 10. If quality is below 0, the captain makes a daily Charisma check plus quality. A total of 1 to 9 lowers quality by 1. 0 or lower means mutiny. In port, quality of 3 or lower rises 1 daily.",
 "Optional Class Features|TCE": "An optional feature is an extra class feature you may gain at its listed level if your DM agrees. You can take any, all, or none. If a feature replaces another, you lose the replaced one and don't qualify for anything that requires it.",
 "Planar Effects|DMG": "Optional per-plane effects. Feywild: a DC 10 Wisdom save on leaving or you lose your memories of it, and time may warp. Shadowfell: a DC 10 Wisdom save against despair, with a DC 15 retry after each long rest. Outer Planes: a DC 10 Constitution save after a long rest on an incompatible plane or gain 1 level of exhaustion. Each also adds its own effect, such as Ysgard's return to life at dawn and Mechanus's average damage.",
 "Player Characters as Lycanthropes|MM": "A lycanthrope PC keeps their statistics and gains the lycanthrope's speeds, damage immunities, traits, and actions not needing equipment. They are proficient with natural attacks and can't speak in animal form. A humanoid hit by a cursed attack must succeed on a Constitution save (DC 8 + the lycanthrope's proficiency bonus + Constitution modifier). Embracing the curse adopts its alignment. Minimum scores: werebear Strength 19, wereboar 17, wererat Dexterity 15, weretiger Strength 17, werewolf Strength 15.",
 "Player Characters as Vampires|MM": "A PC turned into a vampire spawn and then a vampire keeps their statistics, except Strength, Dexterity, and Constitution become 18 if lower. They gain the vampire's damage resistances, darkvision, traits, and actions. Attacks use Strength. The Charm save DC is 8 + proficiency bonus + Charisma modifier. Alignment becomes lawful evil, and the DM may control the character until a wish spell reverses vampirism or the character is killed and revived.",
 "Playing on a Grid|PHB": "Each square is 5 feet. Move square by square, so a speed of 30 feet is 6 squares. Entering a square needs at least 1 square of movement left, even diagonally, and difficult terrain costs 2 squares. Diagonal movement can't cross a wall corner or similar feature. For range, count squares by the shortest route, starting from a square next to one target and ending in the other's space.",
 "Plot Points|DMG": "Optional rule. Each player starts with 1 plot point and can spend 1 per session, a limit the DM can raise. When every player has spent theirs, each gains 1. Options: What a Twist, the player adds a fact the table accepts. The Plot Thickens, the player to their right adds a complication. The Gods Must Be Crazy, there is no permanent DM, and spending a point lets that player become DM while their character becomes an NPC.",
 "Proficiency Dice|DMG": "Optional rule. Roll a die instead of adding your proficiency bonus. Levels 1-4 use 1d4, 5-8 use 1d6, 9-12 use 1d8, 13-16 use 1d10, and 17-20 use 1d12. When a feature doubles proficiency, such as Expertise, roll the die twice. The rule is meant for characters with levels, not monsters.",
 "Prophet's Gambit|COS": "A Tarokka card game for 3 to 5 players. Each player gets 6 Common Deck cards and a hidden Fate card, and a Focus card sits in the center. Over two rounds, players play a card or discard to Risk a High Deck effect. The goal is the best hand from 3 hand cards, the Fate card, and the Focus card. The highest card decides whether to kill or save the Focus.",
 "Random Ships|GOS": "Optional tables to generate ships met at sea. Roll d100 for type: rowboat, keelboat, longship, sailing ship, galley, or warship. Also roll names and a purpose: cargo, passenger, fishing, military, piracy, mercenary, or ghost. Ghost ships resist many damage types, pass through objects as difficult terrain, and take 5 (1d10) force damage ending a turn inside one. Roll d6 for attitude (Friendly, Neutral, Hostile), then crew race and disposition, such as Diseased, Emergency, Mutiny, or Trading.",
 "Renown|DMG": "Optional rule tracking standing in an organization, starting at 0 for each. Gain 1 for advancing its interests and 2 for completing a mission it assigned or that directly benefits it. Thresholds can set ranks (examples at 1, 3, 10, 25, and 50), friendlier attitudes, and perks such as contacts, followers, and favors. Serious offenses reduce it, never below 0. As piety, it measures devotion to gods, and each rank allows praying for divine favor once per day.",
 "Rest Variants|DMG": "Optional rules. Epic Heroism: a short rest takes 5 minutes and a long rest takes 1 hour. Consider restoring only half of spell slots (rounded down), up to 5th level, after such a rest, with a full 8-hour rest restoring all. Gritty Realism: a short rest takes 8 hours and a long rest takes 7 days, which slows the campaign and suits intrigue over frequent combat.",
 "Scroll Mishaps|DMG": "Variant rule. A creature that fails to cast a spell from a spell scroll makes a DC 10 Intelligence save. On a failure, roll d6. 1: take 1d6 force damage per spell level. 2: the spell hits the caster or an ally, or a random nearby target. 3: random location in range. 4: opposite, harmless effect. 5: bizarre effect for the spell's duration or 1d10 minutes. 6: the spell activates after 1d12 hours.",
 "Shared Campaign Variant Rules|XGE": "Variant rules for shared campaigns. Characters earn 1 checkpoint per hour of an adventure's designed length, plus 1 for an adventure of a higher tier. Advance with 4 checkpoints at levels 1-4 and 8 at level 5 or higher. On gaining a level, receive gold: 75 gp at levels 2-4, 150 gp at 5-10, 550 gp at 11-16, 5,500 gp at 17-20, with free lifestyle. Treasure points buy approved magic items. Potions and scrolls can be bought for gold.",
 "Ship Repairs|AAG": "Repairing a berthed ship takes 1 day and 20 gp per hit point. Shipboard weapons take 1 day and 10 gp per hit point. The mending spell restores 1d8 + the caster's spellcasting ability modifier hit points to a ship or shipboard weapon, no more than once per hour.",
 "Ship Stat Blocks|GOS": "Rules for ship stat blocks. A ship has size, space, capacity, travel pace, six ability scores, and usually immunity to poison, psychic, and conditions such as blinded, charmed, frightened, paralyzed, and stunned. It can't act alone, and the captain picks its actions, each once per turn. Components are hull, control, movement, and weapon, each with AC, hit points, and sometimes a damage threshold. A destroyed hull wrecks the ship.",
 "Ship-to-Ship Combat|AAG": "Rules for ship combat. The DM sets starting distance at 250 feet, 500 feet, or 1,000 feet. Initiative Variants suit these fights. Shipboard weapons like ballistae and mangonels load slowly, so characters usually use their own attacks. A spelljammer at the helm moves and steers the ship without spending actions or movement. When a ship moves within 5 feet of another, creatures can cross to board.",
 "Ships in Combat|GOS": "Ships roll initiative with Dexterity plus crew quality. The captain, first mate, or bosun can use an action for Take Aim (advantage on a weapon's next attack) or Full Speed Ahead (add 1d6 times 5 feet to speed). Area spells hit 1d6 crew per spell level. A crashing ship makes a DC 10 Constitution save or its hull takes 1d6 (Small) to 16d10 (Gargantuan) damage. A struck creature makes a Dexterity save, DC 10 + the ship's Strength modifier.",
 "Sidekicks|ESK": "Sidekicks are allied NPCs of three types: Expert, Spellcaster, and Warrior. Each starts at 1st level and levels like a PC, or starts higher at the DM's choice. Experts gain Cunning Action at 2, Expertise at 3, and Extra Attack at 6. Spellcasters gain spells, slots, and Potent Cantrips at 6. Warriors gain Second Wind at 2, Improved Critical at 3, and Extra Attack at 6. Ability Score Improvement comes at 4 and proficiency bonus rises at 5.",
 "Sidekicks|TCE": "Rules to add a sidekick. Any creature with challenge rating 1/2 or lower that befriends a PC can become an Expert, Spellcaster, or Warrior. It starts at the party's average level and levels up whenever that average rises. Each level it gains a Hit Die and hit points (roll plus Constitution modifier, minimum 1). Its proficiency bonus follows its class table. Count each sidekick as a character for encounter difficulty.",
 "Skill Variants|DMG": "Optional rules replacing skills. Ability Check Proficiency: you are proficient in two abilities, one from class and one from background. Background Proficiency: no skill or tool proficiencies, but add proficiency bonus to any check your background reasonably covers, as the DM judges. Personality Trait Proficiency: add proficiency bonus to checks tied to positive personality traits, with disadvantage when a negative trait applies.",
 "Skills with Different Abilities|PHB": "Variant rule. The DM may call for an unusual pairing of ability and skill, or you may ask to apply a proficiency to a different check. If you are proficient, add your proficiency bonus as usual. Examples: Constitution (Athletics) for a long swim, or Strength (Intimidation) for a show of raw strength.",
 "Spell Points|DMG": "Variant rule. Spell points replace slots. Slot costs: 1st 2, 2nd 3, 3rd 5, 4th 6, 5th 7, 6th 9, 7th 10, 8th 11, 9th 13. Regain all points on a long rest. The pool grows from 4 at level 1 to 133 at level 20, capping slot level from 1st to 9th. Make only one slot of each level 6 or higher per long rest. Paladins and rangers halve levels, fighters and rogues divide by 3.",
 "Sphere [Area of Effect]|XPHB": "A Sphere extends outward in all directions from a point of origin. The effect specifies its radius. The point of origin is included in the area.",
 "Superior Ship Upgrades|GOS": "Each upgrade costs 15,000 gp and 1d4 weeks of work in port. If the ship leaves, the work restarts without paying again. Categories: hull, movement, weapon, figurehead, and miscellaneous. A component gains one upgrade, or two if one is Arcane Artillery. A ship can have one figurehead upgrade. Each miscellaneous upgrade can be taken once, such as Smuggler's Banner or Taskmaster's Drums.",
 "Survivors|VRGR": "Optional rule for horror play. Survivors are simple premade characters (Apprentice, Disciple, Sneak, Squire) for one to three session adventures, dreams, or memories. They start at 1st level, and the DM raises levels as the story fits. At 2nd and 3rd level each gains a Survivor Talent, such as Adrenaline Surge, Desperate Scream, Resilience, or Skillful. Each level adds a Hit Die. At 0 hit points they make death saves.",
 "Tool Proficiencies|XGE": "Optional rules for tools. If you are proficient with both a relevant tool and skill, the DM may grant advantage on the check, and possibly extra information or a bonus effect on success. Each tool entry lists components, skill uses, special uses, and sample DCs. Examples: alchemist's supplies make acid or alchemist's fire during a long rest, brewer's supplies purify 6 gallons of water per long rest, and carpenter's tools raise a door's force-open DC by 5.",
 "Travel at Sea|GOS": "Ships can't go faster than their speed. Each 10 feet of speed lost cuts pace by 1 mile per hour and 24 miles per day. Officer activities: Raise Morale (first mate, DC 15 Charisma, +1 quality), Navigate, Repair (bosun, DC 15 Strength), Stealth. Crew passive Perception is 10 + quality. Each day roll d20 and on a 20 a hazard strikes: crew conflict, fire, fog, infestation, or storm, resolved by group checks against DC 10, 15, 20, or 25.",
 "Tying Knots|XGE": "Optional rule. The creature tying a knot makes an Intelligence (Sleight of Hand) check. The total becomes the DC to untie the knot with Intelligence (Sleight of Hand) or slip out of it with Dexterity (Acrobatics).",
 "Wands That Don't Recharge|DMG": "Variant rule. Some wands can be unable to regain charges. Consider raising such a wand's starting charges, up to 25. Spent charges never return.",
 "Activate an Item|DMG": "An action to activate an item isn't Use an Object, so Fast Hands can't activate it. A command word must be spoken aloud and fails in silence. Consumables like potions, oils, and scrolls lose their magic once used. Spells cast from items use the lowest level, spend no slots, need no components, and keep normal casting time, range, duration, and Concentration. Without spellcasting, your modifier is +0 but proficiency bonus applies. Identify or attunement reveals charges.",
 "Climb onto a Bigger Creature|DMG": "Optional rule. A much larger creature can be treated as terrain. The climber uses its action for a Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics) check contested by the target's Dexterity (Acrobatics). On a win it enters the target's space, moves with it, gains advantage on attacks against it, and treats the space as difficult terrain. The target can dislodge it as an action with a Strength (Athletics) check contested by the climber's choice of Athletics or Acrobatics.",
 "Disarm|DMG": "Optional rule. As an attack, you try to knock an item from a target's grasp. Your attack roll is contested by the target's Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics) check. On a win, you deal no damage and the target drops the item. You have disadvantage if the target holds it with two or more hands. The target has advantage on its check if larger than you, or disadvantage if smaller.",
 "Don or Doff a Shield|PHB": "Donning or doffing a shield takes an action. Armor takes longer, per the donning and doffing rules.",
 "Don or Doff a Shield|XPHB": "Donning or doffing a Shield takes an action.",
 "End Concentration|PHB": "A spell needing concentration says so in its Duration entry. You can end concentration at any time and it takes no action.",
 "End Concentration|XPHB": "You can end Concentration at any time. It takes no action.",
 "Escape a Grapple|PHB": "A grappled creature uses its action to make a Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics) check contested by the grappler's Strength (Athletics) check. Success frees it.",
 "Escape a Grapple|XPHB": "A Grappled creature uses its action to make a Strength (Athletics) or Dexterity (Acrobatics) check against the grapple's escape DC. Success ends the condition. It also ends if the grappler has the Incapacitated condition or if the distance between them exceeds the grapple's range.",
 "Healing Surge|DMG": "Optional rule. As an action, spend up to half your Hit Dice. Roll each die and add your Constitution modifier to regain that many hit points. You may spend extra dice after each roll. You can't use this again until a short or long rest. A long rest restores all spent Hit Dice. A short rest restores Hit Dice equal to your level divided by 4, minimum 1.",
 "Identify a Spell|XGE": "If you perceived the casting, the effect, or both, you can use your reaction to identify a spell as it is cast, or an action on your turn to identify it by its effect afterward. Make an Intelligence (Arcana) check against DC 15 + the spell's level. You have advantage if the spell was cast as a spell of your own class. Your own spellcasting ability does not matter.",
 "Improvising an Action|PHB": "You can attempt actions not listed, such as breaking down doors or intimidating enemies. The limits are your imagination and your ability scores. Describe the action, and the DM says whether it is possible and what roll, if any, decides success.",
 "Improvising an Action|XPHB": "Characters and monsters can improvise actions not covered elsewhere. Describe the action, and the DM says whether it is possible and what D20 Test it needs, if any.",
 "Mark|DMG": "Optional rule. When you make a melee attack, you can also mark its target. Until the end of your next turn, your opportunity attacks against the marked target have advantage and don't use your reaction. You can't make the attack if something stops you taking reactions, like the incapacitated condition or shocking grasp. You get one opportunity attack per turn.",
 "Other Activity|PHB": "On your turn you can speak briefly and gesture for free. You can also interact with one object or feature for free during your move or action, such as opening a door or drawing a weapon as part of an attack. A second object needs your action. The DM may require an action for tasks needing special care, like opening a stuck door or cranking a drawbridge. Some magic items always need an action.",
 "Overrun|DMG": "Optional rule. As an action or bonus action, you try to move through a hostile creature's space with a Strength (Athletics) check contested by its Strength (Athletics). You have advantage if larger, or disadvantage if smaller. On a win you can move through its space once this turn.",
 "Shove Aside|DMG": "Optional rule. Use the shove attack to push a target sideways instead of away, with disadvantage on your Strength (Athletics) check. On a success, you move the target 5 feet to a different space within your reach.",
 "Stabilize a Creature|PHB": "Use your action to give first aid to an unconscious creature with a DC 10 Wisdom (Medicine) check. A stable creature makes no death saves and stays unconscious at 0 hit points. It stops being stable if it takes any damage. If not healed, it regains 1 hit point after 1d4 hours.",
 "Tumble|DMG": "Optional rule. As an action or bonus action, make a Dexterity (Acrobatics) check contested by a hostile creature's Dexterity (Acrobatics). On a win, you move through its space once this turn.",
 "Waking Someone|XGE": "A naturally sleeping creature wakes if it takes damage, if someone uses an action to shake or slap it, or from a loud noise like yelling or thunder. Whispers don't wake it unless its passive Wisdom (Perception) is 20 or higher and they come within 10 feet. Normal speech wakes it in a quiet area if its passive Wisdom (Perception) is 15 or higher.",
}
